{{also|B-lister}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|blister}}, from {{der|en|fro|blestre}}, from a {{der|en|gem}} source. Compare {{cog|dum|blyster|t=swelling}}, {{cog|non|blastr|t=a blowing}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈblɪstə(ɹ)/|a=UK}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-blister.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈblɪstɚ/|a=GA}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪstə(ɹ)|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{senseid|en|Q276469}}A small [[bubble]] between the layers of the [[skin]] that contains watery or bloody fluid and is caused by [[friction]] and pressure, [[burn]]ing, freezing, chemical irritation, disease{{,}} or [[infect]]ion.
#: {{hypo|en|bulla|vesicle|vesicula}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1967|author=Donald Howard Grainger|title=Don't Die in the Bundu|passage=Inspect them for rub marks and '''blisters'''; tape or bandage rub marks; clean the skin around a '''blister''', use a sterilised needle to puncture it at its outer edge and press out the fluid, then bandage.}}
# A swelling on a plant.
# {{lb|en|medicine}} Something applied to the skin to raise a blister; a [[vesicatory]] or other applied medicine.
#* {{RQ:Byron Don Juan|passage='T is written in the Hebrew Chronicle, / How the physicians, leaving pill and potion, / Prescribed, by way of '''blister''', a young belle, / When old King David's blood grew dull in motion, / And that the medicine answered very well {{...}}}}
# A bubble, as on a painted surface.
# {{lb|en|roofing}} An [[enclosed]] pocket of air, which may be mixed with water or [[solvent]] [[vapor]], trapped between [[impermeable]] layers of felt or between the [[membrane]] and [[substrate]].
# A type of pre-formed packaging made from plastic that contains cavities.
#: {{co|en|'''blister''' card}}
#: {{co|en|'''blister''' pack}}
# A cause of annoyance.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1923|author=Pelham Grenville Wodehouse|url=https://books.google.ca/books?id=3Lw_AQAAIAAJ|title=The Inimitable Jeeves|page=39
|passage=I couldn't help thinking how dashed happy I could have contrived to be in this place if only Aunt Agatha and the other '''blisters''' had been elsewhere.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1933|url=https://books.google.ca/books?id=G0EfAQAAMAAJ|journal=Collier's Illustrated Weekly|volume=91|page=14
|passage=I will say, however, that we fanned her well — her and her old '''blister''' of a mother and a bewhiskered old goat named Boris.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2013|author=P.G. Wodehouse|title=Blandings: TV Tie-In|pageurl=https://books.google.ca/books?id=ViXOWq4fhWoC&pg=PA126|page=126
|passage='We mustn't laugh about it, my boy. It's no joking matter. It's very wrong to shoot Mr Baxter.'<br>'But he's a '''blister'''.'<br>'He is a '''blister''',' agreed Lord Emsworth, always fairminded. 'Nevertheless. . . . Remember, he is your tutor.'}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2017|author=Joe Archibald|title=The Willie Klump MEGAPACK®|pageurl=https://books.google.ca/books?id=30UwDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA302|page=302
|passage=Willie suddenly realized the heat really wasn't off the criminal persons, and he sprang into action. The blonde '''blister''' also recovered surprisingly fast and threw the big wordy tome at the Klump coco .}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} A form of [[smelt]]ed [[copper]] with a blistered [[surface]].

